# [[ project_slug ]]

The [[ project_slug ]] API module: an HTTP server with configuration, structured
logging, and permission-gated route registration, plus a SvelteKit PWA in
`ui/`.

[[ project_name ]] was generated from the
[go-web-base](https://github.com/andrewmyhre/go-web-base) Copier template.
`.copier-answers.yml` records the answers, and `uvx copier update` merges in
later template changes. `llms.txt` lists every doc; the example role graph is
`docs/roles.md`.

## Stack

- [chi](https://github.com/go-chi/chi) for HTTP routing, with
  [Huma](https://huma.rocks) on top (through its `humachi` adapter) for
  typed operations and a generated OpenAPI 3.1 spec.
- [zap](https://github.com/uber-go/zap) for structured JSON logging,
  including the per-request access log and panic recovery (chi
  middleware).
- [Cobra](https://github.com/spf13/cobra) for the command line.
- [Viper](https://github.com/spf13/viper) for configuration.

## Layout

- `config/`: server configuration (address, log level, the database and
  Redis connection strings, and the Google OAuth client), read by Viper from
  `[[ env_prefix ]]_`-prefixed environment variables and the `serve` flags.
- `logger/`: the zap JSON logger.
- `redis/`: the Redis client and a `Store` with per-key expiry, used to hold
  the one-shot OAuth state records and the sessions.
- `server/`: the HTTP server, the permission-gated router, the zap
  request-logging and recovery middleware, and the Google OAuth sign-in with
  its session middleware.
- `cmd/[[ project_slug ]]/`: the `[[ project_slug ]]` binary and its route table.

## Route permissions

Every route is a Huma operation that declares the permission a caller
needs to invoke it:

```go
type HealthOutput struct {
    Body struct {
        Status string `json:"status"`
    }
}

server.Register(router, server.Operation{
    OperationID: "get-health",
    Method:      http.MethodGet,
    Path:        "/health",
    Permission:  server.Public,
}, func(ctx context.Context, _ *struct{}) (*HealthOutput, error) {
    out := &HealthOutput{}
    out.Body.Status = "ok"
    return out, nil
})
```

Registration without a valid permission is refused, and the server
re-checks every route when it starts. A route without a declared
permission can therefore never be served: an unprotected route cannot
exist.

### Permissions are typed

A `server.Permission` is a `Resource` and a `Verb`, in the form the Roles
section of `docs/roles.md` uses (`String()` gives
`"Projects: Read"`):

```go
server.Permission{Resource: server.Projects, Verb: server.Read}
```

- Resources are the 14 docs/roles.md names: `Projects`,
  `AgentConversations`, `AgentMessages`, `PermissionPrompts`, `Requests`,
  `ProjectFiles`, `Workflows`, `WorkflowSteps`, `Secrets`,
  `UserInvitation`, `WorkflowLink`, `Users`, `AccessReviews` and
  `Providers`.
- Verbs are `Read`, `Create`, `Patch` and `Delete` on any resource, plus
  two that apply to one resource only: `Answer` (only
  `PermissionPrompts: Answer`) and `Run` (only `Workflows: Run`, which the
  roles doc grants to Contributors although its verb list omits it).
- `server.Public` declares a route that needs no role, such as `/health`
  and `/openapi.json`. It is a deliberate declaration: the zero value
  `Permission{}` is refused with `ErrNoPermission`, and only this package
  can construct `Public`. `p.IsPublic()` tells an authorisation middleware
  to let the route through.

`Register` and `Build` refuse the zero value (`ErrNoPermission`), an
unknown resource (`ErrUnknownResource`), an unknown verb
(`ErrUnknownVerb`) and a one-resource verb on any other resource
(`ErrVerbNotAllowed`).

`server.Register` is the only way to add a route. The chi router and the
`huma.API` are built inside `Router.Build` and never handed out (`Build`
returns a plain `http.Handler`), so no caller can register an unchecked
route. The declared `Permission` value is stored in the operation's Huma
`Metadata` under `permission`, is available to handlers through
`server.PermissionFromContext`, and is recorded in the access log in its
`String()` form (`"Projects: Read"` or `"public"`).

### The OpenAPI spec and Huma's own routes

Huma normally registers its own routes (`/openapi.json`, `/openapi.yaml`
and the 3.0 variants, `/docs` and `/schemas/{schema}`) directly on the
router, outside `server.Register`. Those are all switched off. The spec is
served instead at `GET /openapi.json` as an ordinary registration
declared `server.Public`, so it goes through the same check as every
other route. There are no exempt routes: public is a declaration, not an
absence.

As a last check, `Build` walks the chi router and refuses to start if it
holds any route that did not come through a permission-checked
registration. Turning Huma's automatic routes back on therefore stops the
server at startup instead of serving them unchecked.

### Duplicates and conflicts

chi does not panic on a conflicting pattern such as `/things/{name}` next
to `/things/{id}`: it silently replaces the earlier route. `Register`
refuses a method and path that match an existing route once parameter
names are ignored, and `Build` refuses to start if any checked route is
missing from the router afterwards. Routes chi or Huma panic on (for
example an unclosed `{` or a non-struct input type) come back from `Build`
as errors.

## Sign-in (Google OAuth)

Users sign in with Google's OpenID Connect authorization-code flow, with
PKCE (S256) and a nonce. The Google account ID (the ID token's `sub`) is the
identity; the email is shown, never matched on. Sign-in is off while
`[[ env_prefix ]]_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` is empty, and needs Redis when it is on: `serve`
refuses to start with a client id and no `[[ env_prefix ]]_REDIS_ADDR`.

| route | permission | behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /auth/google` | public | records a sign-in attempt, sets the state cookie, 302 to Google |
| `GET /auth/google/callback` | public | checks and consumes the attempt, verifies the ID token, starts a session, 302 to `/` (the UI) |
| `POST /auth/logout` | public | deletes the session and clears its cookie, 204 |
| `GET /me` | authenticated | the signed-in user's `id`, `name`, `email` and `avatar`, or 401 |

### The sign-in attempt

`GET /auth/google` stores a PKCE verifier and a nonce in Redis under a random
256-bit id with a 10 minute TTL. That id is the OAuth `state`, and it is also
set in the `[[ ident ]]_oauth_state` cookie (`Path=/auth/google`). The callback
refuses a `state` that does not equal the cookie, so an attempt only
completes in the browser that started it, and takes the attempt with
`GETDEL`, so it completes at most once.

The code is exchanged with the verifier. The ID token from the token
endpoint is verified with go-oidc: its RS256 signature against Google's JWKS,
`iss`, `aud` equal to the client id, and `exp`; then `nonce` must equal the
attempt's and `azp`, when present, the client id. The email is kept only when
`email_verified` is true.

### Sessions

A successful callback ends any session the browser already had and creates a
new one under a fresh random 256-bit id, so a planted id never becomes a
signed-in session. The session lives in Redis for 7 days from sign-in, not
refreshed on use. The `[[ ident ]]_session` cookie carries only the id and is
`HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Lax` and `Path=/`.

There is no return-to parameter: the callback always lands on `/`, the UI home page, so
there is no open redirect.

### Authorization

A route that is not `server.Public` needs a session: without one it answers
401, and with an unreachable Redis 503. `server.Authenticated` routes (like
`/me`) need nothing more. Any other permission is checked with Casbin in the
global domain, 403 on a denial. Every check of a non-public route logs one
`rbac` line, allow or deny (`docs/logging.md`). `Build` refuses to start a
router holding a non-public route it has no session store for, or a role
route it has no authorizer for.

### Configuration

| setting | flag | environment |
|---|---|---|
| Google client id | `--google-client-id` | `[[ env_prefix ]]_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` |
| Google client secret | none | `[[ env_prefix ]]_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` |
| Google redirect URI | `--google-redirect-uri` | `[[ env_prefix ]]_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI` |
| Redis address | `--redis-addr` | `[[ env_prefix ]]_REDIS_ADDR` |
| Redis password | none | `[[ env_prefix ]]_REDIS_PASSWORD` |

The client secret and Redis password are environment only, so they never
appear in process arguments. The redirect URI is required and must be
registered with the Google OAuth client; it is never derived from a
request's `Host` header. With `[[ env_prefix ]]_DATABASE_DSN` set, `serve` applies the
migrations at startup, then builds the Casbin enforcer and binds
`[[ env_prefix ]]_ADMIN_SUB` (a Google account ID) to Admin.

## Running

```sh
go run ./cmd/[[ project_slug ]] serve
go run ./cmd/[[ project_slug ]] serve --addr :9090 --log-level debug
[[ env_prefix ]]_ADDR=:9090 [[ env_prefix ]]_LOG_LEVEL=debug go run ./cmd/[[ project_slug ]] serve
```

| setting   | flag          | environment        | default |
|-----------|---------------|--------------------|---------|
| address   | `--addr`      | `[[ env_prefix ]]_ADDR`      | `:8080` |
| log level | `--log-level` | `[[ env_prefix ]]_LOG_LEVEL` | `info`  |

The database, admin-sub, Redis and Google OAuth settings are environment
variables (and some, like `--google-client-id`, also flags); see the
[Sign-in](#sign-in-google-oauth) section for the full table.

A flag given on the command line wins over the environment, which wins
over the default. Log levels are `debug`, `info`, `warn` and `error`.

